1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:00,980
<u>Android basics</u>

2
00:00:02,297 --> 00:00:04,607
<i>Name the four main Android application components</i>

3
00:00:05,866 --> 00:00:08,936
Activity, Service, BroadcastReceiver, and ContentProvider.

4
00:00:09,636 --> 00:00:11,536
They can serve as application entry points.

5
00:00:12,226 --> 00:00:15,146
Activities, services, and providers are declared in the manifest;

6
00:00:15,346 --> 00:00:17,216
receivers can also be registered at runtime.

7
00:00:18,734 --> 00:00:21,004
<i>How is a Bound Service different from a regular Service?</i>

8
00:00:22,539 --> 00:00:23,699
A Bound Service is a Service

9
00:00:23,719 --> 00:00:27,219
that other components connect to through bindService() and interact with through an IBinder.

10
00:00:27,929 --> 00:00:30,689
A purely bound service exists while at least one client is bound.

11
00:00:31,399 --> 00:00:34,309
A started service can continue independently of the component that started it

12
00:00:34,499 --> 00:00:37,249
, subject to process death and Android background execution limits.

13
00:00:38,761 --> 00:00:41,581
<i>Which is initialized first: Application or ContentProvider?</i>

14
00:00:43,089 --> 00:00:46,889
The Application object is created first, then ContentProvider instances are initialized,

15
00:00:47,059 --> 00:00:49,409
and only after that Application.onCreate() is called.

16
00:00:50,109 --> 00:00:53,349
This is why libraries often use providers for early auto-initialization.

17
00:00:54,886 --> 00:00:57,526
<i>What is the difference between an explicit and an implicit Intent?</i>

18
00:00:59,051 --> 00:01:01,571
An explicit Intent names the exact component to start.

19
00:01:02,281 --> 00:01:07,181
An implicit Intent describes an action, and Android selects a matching application or component through intent filters.

20
00:01:09,057 --> 00:01:09,817
<i>What is a Bundle?</i>

21
00:01:11,066 --> 00:01:15,456
A Bundle is a container for passing small sets of mixed data between Android components and for saving state.

22
00:01:16,146 --> 00:01:20,256
It supports primitive types, Parcelable, Serializable, and several other supported types.

23
00:01:21,769 --> 00:01:24,159
<i>What is the difference between Parcelable and Serializable?</i>

24
00:01:25,694 --> 00:01:31,314
Parcelable is optimized for Android IPC and is usually faster, but it requires a special implementation or @Parcelize.

25
00:01:32,024 --> 00:01:35,464
Serializable is simpler, but it uses reflection and has more overhead.

26
00:01:36,977 --> 00:01:38,587
<i>What happens when the device rotates?</i>

27
00:01:40,110 --> 00:01:44,490
Rotation is a configuration change, and by default the current Activity is destroyed and recreated.

28
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:46,800
A ViewModel survives this recreation,

29
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:50,480
while small UI state can be preserved with SavedStateHandle or onSaveInstanceState().

30
00:01:51,993 --> 00:01:53,413
<i>Can onDestroy() be skipped?</i>

31
00:01:54,915 --> 00:01:55,385
Yes.

32
00:01:56,075 --> 00:01:58,505
The system may terminate the process without calling onDestroy(),

33
00:01:58,725 --> 00:02:01,145
so critical data must not rely on this callback being called.

34
00:02:02,665 --> 00:02:04,595
<i>How does ViewModel survive device rotation?</i>

35
00:02:06,134 --> 00:02:11,164
A ViewModel is stored in a ViewModelStore associated with the logical screen owner rather than a specific Activity instance.

36
00:02:11,864 --> 00:02:16,124
After recreation, the new Activity receives the same ViewModel as long as the process has not been killed.

37
00:02:18,017 --> 00:02:19,557
<i>How many Loopers can one thread have?</i>

38
00:02:20,818 --> 00:02:23,238
A thread can have at most one Looper, and it may have none.

39
00:02:23,938 --> 00:02:25,568
The main thread receives one automatically,

40
00:02:25,748 --> 00:02:28,698
while a background thread can use Looper.prepare() or HandlerThread.

41
00:02:30,201 --> 00:02:33,071
<i>Which Activity lifecycle methods are called during device rotation?</i>

42
00:02:34,582 --> 00:02:38,732
With a normal configuration change, the old Activity goes through onPause() → onStop() → onDestroy(),

43
00:02:38,882 --> 00:02:41,242
and the new one goes through onCreate() → onStart() → onResume().

44
00:02:41,952 --> 00:02:44,812
onSaveInstanceState() is also usually called before destruction.

45
00:02:46,345 --> 00:02:48,995
<i>What is the difference between invalidate() and requestLayout()?</i>

46
00:02:50,515 --> 00:02:53,335
invalidate() requests a redraw and causes a new draw pass.

47
00:02:54,045 --> 00:02:57,705
requestLayout() starts a new measure and layout pass when size or positioning has changed.

48
00:02:59,230 --> 00:03:01,290
<i>Which methods are overridden in a custom ViewGroup?</i>

49
00:03:02,819 --> 00:03:07,039
The main methods are onMeasure() for calculating sizes and onLayout() for positioning child views.

50
00:03:07,749 --> 00:03:10,659
onDraw() is needed only when the container draws its own content.

51
00:03:12,177 --> 00:03:13,447
<i>How does RecyclerView work?</i>

52
00:03:14,974 --> 00:03:18,814
RecyclerView coordinates a LayoutManager, Recycler, Adapter, and ViewHolder.

53
00:03:19,524 --> 00:03:23,174
The LayoutManager decides which item positions are needed and where their views should be placed.

54
00:03:23,874 --> 00:03:25,394
It requests views through the Recycler,

55
00:03:25,584 --> 00:03:29,354
which reuses an existing ViewHolder when possible or asks the Adapter to create and bind one:

56
00:03:29,574 --> 00:03:33,124
RecyclerView -> LayoutManager -> Recycler -> Adapter -> ViewHolder.

57
00:03:34,993 --> 00:03:37,193
<i>What is the difference between ViewBinding and DataBinding?</i>

58
00:03:38,778 --> 00:03:42,388
ViewBinding provides type-safe access to views with very little runtime complexity.

59
00:03:43,088 --> 00:03:48,748
DataBinding additionally supports expressions in XML, two-way binding, and BindingAdapters, but it makes builds more complex.

60
00:03:49,994 --> 00:03:52,314
<i>Activity Context or Application Context?</i>

61
00:03:53,819 --> 00:03:56,679
Use an Activity Context for UI that needs the screen's theme or window,

62
00:03:56,909 --> 00:03:58,609
such as dialogs and themed view inflation.

63
00:03:59,319 --> 00:04:03,519
Use the Application Context for process-scoped dependencies that do not need that UI configuration.

64
00:04:05,044 --> 00:04:06,004
<i>What is an ANR?</i>

65
00:04:07,539 --> 00:04:12,069
ANR means that an app component missed a system response deadline, often because the main thread is blocked.

66
00:04:12,779 --> 00:04:17,359
Common causes are blocking operations, heavy computations, and incorrect synchronization on the main thread.

67
00:04:18,894 --> 00:04:19,614
<i>What is jank?</i>

68
00:04:21,134 --> 00:04:23,774
Jank is visible stutter caused by a frame missing its deadline.

69
00:04:24,484 --> 00:04:28,284
At 60 FPS, the budget for one frame is about 16.6 ms.

70
00:04:29,801 --> 00:04:30,701
<i>What is overdraw?</i>

71
00:04:32,219 --> 00:04:35,699
Overdraw happens when the same pixels are drawn multiple times on top of each other.

72
00:04:36,409 --> 00:04:40,556
It is not always a problem, but unnecessary backgrounds and layers can create extra GPU work.
